#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""shadowfetch-bundle-install - privileged catalog/bundle helper for Ignition.

This is the single root-side entry point for Ignition Setup and for the
Bundles surfaces in Welcome and Control Center.  It has exactly three verbs
and refuses free-form arguments: catalog ids resolve only against the
root-owned records in /usr/share/shadowfetch/welcome/catalog/.

  install <catalog-id>      Two-stage install of the record's package list.
                            polkit action: org.shadowfetch.bundle-install
                            (auth_admin_keep - this is a system mutation).

  save-choice <catalog-id>  Record an offline bundle choice so it can be
                            offered again the moment the machine connects.
                            polkit action: org.shadowfetch.ignition-state
                            (allow_active=yes - writing a 200-byte choice
                            file is not a system mutation, so the offline
                            path never asks for a password).

  mark-done                 Stamp Ignition as completed.  This is how the
                            zero-package Core pick finishes: passwordless,
                            zero downloads, zero prompts.
                            polkit action: org.shadowfetch.ignition-state.

The save-choice and mark-done verbs are reached through the separate
/usr/libexec/shadowfetch-ignition-state entry point (pkexec maps one polkit
action to one program path); that wrapper re-execs this file with
--state-entry, which restricts the verb set.

MACHINE PROTOCOL (stdout, one event per line - consumed by the Welcome UI):

  SF-LOG <path>                    log file for this run
  SF-STAGE download|commit         a stage began
  SF-PROGRESS <stage> <pct> <msg>  progress within a stage
  SF-LOCKWAIT <hint>               waiting for the apt/dpkg lock
  SF-LOCKFREE                      the lock became free, continuing
  SF-POINT <n>                     the Phoenix Point (first pre snapshot of
                                   this transaction) protecting the install.
                                   Emitted only for a Point that has been
                                   RECORDED in Fireproof's update record, so
                                   the number offered here and the number
                                   `fireproof rollback` restores are one
                                   number, not two.
  SF-NOPOINT <reason>              no Phoenix Point is offered.  reason is
                                   one of: no-authority (shadowfetch-fireproof,
                                   which owns the one snapshot and state
                                   implementation, is not installed),
                                   not-btrfs, unavailable (btrfs, but snapper
                                   cannot produce a Point here), no-snapshot
                                   (a Point should have appeared and did not),
                                   pending-update (an update is still waiting
                                   on a good boot, and its Point outranks this
                                   one), not-recorded (a Point exists but could
                                   not be written to the update record, so
                                   nothing may offer it)
  SF-AUDIT-DIRTY                   dpkg --audit is non-empty after a failure
  SF-CANCELLED                     the user cancelled during stage 1
  SF-DONE                          success
  SF-ERROR <code> <message>        failure

Stage 1 (download) is apt-get -d: cancelling it or losing the network
changes nothing on disk and partial debs resume.  Stage 2 (commit) installs
from the local cache, ignores cancel requests, and is wrapped in automatic
Phoenix Points by the shipped 79phoenix-space + 80snapper DPkg hooks - this
helper creates no snapshots of its own.

ONE SNAPSHOT AUTHORITY, AND THIS PROGRAM IS NOT IT.

This is the second program in the distribution that commits packages as root,
and it has to be: fireproofd's only verb is the full upgrade of everything
upgradable, and a NAMED CATALOG BUNDLE is not that.  What must not happen is a
second system DECIDING snapshot behaviour.  Stage U deleted exactly that shape
from shadowfetch-update, where a second updater derived a Point with its own
awk, relabelled the Point Fireproof labels, and recorded it NOWHERE - so
/var/lib/shadowfetch/fireproof-state.json still described the previous
transaction and `fireproof rollback` would have restored the wrong system.

This file carried two thirds of that same defect, and it was measured before
it was fixed: with a previous Fireproof update recorded at Point 41, a bundle
install printed SF-POINT 43 (its own transaction's Point, which the Welcome
failure card turns into `pkexec /usr/libexec/phoenix-restore 43`) while
fireproof-state.json stayed byte-for-byte unchanged and Fireproof1.RollbackTarget
still answered `pkexec /usr/libexec/phoenix-restore 41`.  Two programs, two
different answers to "what does the rollback button restore".  It never
relabelled, which is the one third it did not have.

So:

  * the Point comes ONLY from sfupdate.snapshots, the single implementation
    Stage U extracted.  There is no snapper parser here and no snapper
    invocation here.
  * NOTHING is relabelled.  sfupdate.snapshots.label_point is the only snapper
    mutation in the product and it belongs to fireproofd.  A bundle install's
    Point keeps the description snapper's own 80snapper hook wrote, which is
    the honest one.
  * the transaction is RECORDED through sfupdate.state - the one store, in the
    shape fireproofd writes - BEFORE the Point is offered to anybody.  A Point
    this program cannot record is a Point it does not name.
  * fireproof-pending is NOT written.  That flag means "a commit is waiting on
    a good boot to be judged", and fireproof-postboot restores from it when a
    desktop does not come back.  An update still under that assessment has to
    keep priority: if the desktop breaks after an update AND a later bundle
    install, the Point that repairs it is the update's, not the bundle's.

When shadowfetch-fireproof is not installed there is no authority to record
into, and this program then offers NO Point at all (SF-NOPOINT no-authority)
rather than a number nothing has recorded.  That is the whole defect, and
printing it anyway would be keeping it.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 2 usage, 3 bad id/record, 4 not enough disk space,
5 download failed, 6 commit failed, 7 privilege error, 10 cancelled.

Zero telemetry: the only network activity is apt talking to the mirrors
already configured on this system, and only inside the install verb.
"""

import fcntl
import grp
import hashlib
import json
import os
import pwd
import re
import select
import signal
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import uuid
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path

SOURCE = "shadowfetch-bundle-install"

CATALOG_DIR = "/usr/share/shadowfetch/welcome/catalog"
STATE_DIR = "/var/lib/shadowfetch"
LOG_DIR = "/var/log/shadowfetch"
DONE_FILE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "ignition-done")
CHOICE_FILE = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "ignition-choice.json")
LIMITS_FILE = "/etc/security/limits.d/95-shadowfetch-audio.conf"


def _load_update_authority():
    """Import sfupdate - the ONE snapshot and state implementation - or None.

    Installed, this file is /usr/libexec/shadowfetch-bundle-install and the
    modules are /usr/lib/shadowfetch/{sfupdate,phoenix}, so the search starts
    at this file's own location.  Never PYTHONPATH: the caller controls that
    and this program runs as root.

    The source-tree fallback is only consulted when the installed directory
    does not hold sfupdate, because in the source tree the two live in
    different package directories.  It cannot fire on a real system - the
    directories it looks for would have to be created under /usr/libexec, /usr
    or / and all three already require root.

    None is an honest answer: shadowfetch-welcome does not depend on
    shadowfetch-fireproof, so a machine can legitimately have Welcome without
    the update authority.  Every caller below turns None into "no Point is
    offered", never into a Point derived some other way.
    """
    here = Path(__file__).resolve()
    installed = here.parent.parent / "lib" / "shadowfetch"
    roots = [installed]
    if not (installed / "sfupdate").is_dir():
        for parent in here.parents:
            roots.append(parent
                         / "packages/shadowfetch-fireproof/data/usr/lib/shadowfetch")
            roots.append(parent / "packages/shadowfetch-phoenix/usr/lib/shadowfetch")
    found = [str(root) for root in roots
             if root.is_dir() and str(root) not in sys.path]
    sys.path[:0] = found
    try:
        from sfupdate import snapshots, state
    except ImportError:
        return None, None
    return snapshots, state


sfsnap, sfstate = _load_update_authority()

#: The one update record.  Named here so the whole file spells it one way and
#: a test can point the harness somewhere else; the DEFAULT is the authority's
#: own constant, never a second opinion about where the record lives.
STATE_FILE = sfstate.STATE_FILE if sfstate is not None else None
PENDING_FILE = sfstate.PENDING_FILE if sfstate is not None else None

ID_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$")
PKG_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9+.-]+$")

# Locks apt/dpkg take; we probe them politely before starting so a running
# Discover/pkcon/terminal-apt transaction surfaces as a friendly wait
# message instead of a hard error.
LOCK_FILES = (
    "/var/lib/dpkg/lock-frontend",
    "/var/lib/dpkg/lock",
    "/var/cache/apt/archives/lock",
)

APT_ENV = dict(
    os.environ,
    DEBIAN_FRONTEND="noninteractive",
    LC_ALL="C.UTF-8",
    PATH="/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin",
)


# PERMANENT INVARIANT: an executable that establishes, enforces or attests a
# security fact is named by an explicit ABSOLUTE path and its child PATH is
# pinned too.  Pinning the child PATH (APT_ENV above) was already here; the
# programs themselves were still bare names, resolved by whatever PATH this
# root process inherited.  Each of these decides something:
#
#   apt-get   performs the root package transaction itself
#   dpkg      --audit decides whether a previous transaction left the system
#             broken, i.e. whether a repair runs and whether SF-AUDIT-DIRTY is
#             reported - the forged-clean-scan shape, one layer down
#   gpasswd   grants membership of `pipewire`, which the limits file below
#             gives realtime priority 95 and unlimited memory locking
APT_GET_PATHS = ("/usr/bin/apt-get", "/bin/apt-get")
DPKG_PATHS = ("/usr/bin/dpkg", "/bin/dpkg")
GPASSWD_PATHS = ("/usr/bin/gpasswd", "/usr/sbin/gpasswd", "/bin/gpasswd")


def trusted_program(candidates):
    """First candidate that is a root-owned, non-group/other-writable file.

    Deliberately not shutil.which and deliberately not $PATH: a user-writable
    directory must never be able to answer, and a name that PATH resolves is
    exactly such an answer.  None means "no trusted one exists", which callers
    turn into a refusal - never into a fallback.
    """
    for candidate in candidates:
        try:
            st = os.lstat(candidate)
        except OSError:
            continue
        if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode) or st.st_uid != 0:
            continue
        if st.st_mode & (stat.S_IWGRP | stat.S_IWOTH):
            continue
        return candidate
    return None


def emit(name, payload=""):
    line = "SF-" + name + ((" " + payload) if payload else "")
    print(line, flush=True)


def die(exit_code, err_code, message):
    emit("ERROR", err_code + " " + message)
    sys.exit(exit_code)


def usage():
    print(__doc__, file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)


# ---- catalog record resolution ------------------------------------------

def load_record(catalog_id):
    """Resolve a catalog id against the root-owned catalog dir, strictly."""
    if not ID_RE.match(catalog_id):
        die(3, "bad-id", "catalog id contains invalid characters")
    path = os.path.join(CATALOG_DIR, catalog_id + ".json")
    try:
        st = os.stat(path)
    except OSError:
        die(3, "no-record", "no such catalog entry: " + catalog_id)
    # The record must be root-owned and not writable by group/other: ids
    # resolve only against files the package manager put there.
    if st.st_uid != 0 or (st.st_mode & (stat.S_IWGRP | stat.S_IWOTH)):
        die(3, "bad-record", "catalog entry is not root-owned read-only")
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            rec = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        die(3, "bad-record", "catalog entry is not valid JSON")
    if not isinstance(rec, dict) or rec.get("id") != catalog_id:
        die(3, "bad-record", "catalog entry id mismatch")
    if rec.get("kind") not in ("apt", "preset"):
        die(3, "bad-record", "catalog entry is not an installable kind")
    pkgs = rec.get("packages")
    if not isinstance(pkgs, list):
        die(3, "bad-record", "catalog entry has no package list")
    for p in pkgs:
        if not isinstance(p, str) or not PKG_RE.match(p):
            die(3, "bad-record", "catalog entry names an invalid package")
    return rec


# ---- state files ---------------------------------------------------------

def ensure_dirs():
    for d in (STATE_DIR, LOG_DIR):
        os.makedirs(d, mode=0o755, exist_ok=True)


def atomic_write(path, text, mode=0o644):
    tmp = path + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write(text)
        fh.flush()
        os.fsync(fh.fileno())
    os.chmod(tmp, mode)
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def verb_save_choice(catalog_id):
    rec = load_record(catalog_id)
    ensure_dirs()
    choice = {
        "id": rec["id"],
        "name": rec.get("name") or rec["id"],
        "chosen_at": datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds"),
        "download_bytes": rec.get("size_bytes"),
    }
    atomic_write(CHOICE_FILE, json.dumps(choice) + "\n")
    emit("DONE")


def verb_mark_done():
    ensure_dirs()
    atomic_write(
        DONE_FILE,
        "completed " + datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds") + "\n",
    )
    try:
        os.unlink(CHOICE_FILE)
    except OSError:
        pass
    emit("DONE")


# ---- cancel handling -----------------------------------------------------

def cancel_requested_nonblock():
    """True when the UI wrote CANCEL on our stdin (no blocking)."""
    try:
        r, _, _ = select.select([sys.stdin.fileno()], [], [], 0)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return False
    if not r:
        return False
    try:
        data = os.read(sys.stdin.fileno(), 4096)
    except OSError:
        return False
    return b"CANCEL" in data


# ---- lock politeness -----------------------------------------------------

def wait_for_locks(allow_cancel):
    """Wait until no other package manager holds the dpkg/apt locks."""
    waited = False
    while True:
        busy = False
        for path in LOCK_FILES:
            try:
                fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT, 0o640)
            except OSError:
                continue
            try:
                fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
            except OSError:
                busy = True
            finally:
                os.close(fd)
            if busy:
                break
        if not busy:
            if waited:
                emit("LOCKFREE")
            return True
        if not waited:
            emit("LOCKWAIT", "another package manager is running")
            waited = True
        if allow_cancel and cancel_requested_nonblock():
            emit("CANCELLED")
            return False
        time.sleep(2)


# ---- apt with APT::Status-Fd progress ------------------------------------

def parse_status_line(line, stage):
    # dlstatus:<n>:<pct>:<message>   pmstatus:<pkg>:<pct>:<message>
    parts = line.split(":", 3)
    if len(parts) < 3:
        return
    kind = parts[0]
    if kind not in ("dlstatus", "pmstatus"):
        return
    try:
        pct = float(parts[2])
    except ValueError:
        return
    msg = parts[3].strip() if len(parts) > 3 else ""
    emit("PROGRESS", "%s %.1f %s" % (stage, pct, msg))


def run_apt(argv, stage, log_fh, allow_cancel):
    """Run apt-get with a status fd, streaming progress. -> (rc, cancelled)."""
    rfd, wfd = os.pipe()
    cmd = argv + ["-o", "APT::Status-Fd=%d" % wfd]
    log_fh.write("+ " + " ".join(cmd) + "\n")
    log_fh.flush()
    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(
            cmd,
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stdout=log_fh,
            stderr=log_fh,
            pass_fds=(wfd,),
            env=APT_ENV,
        )
    except OSError as exc:
        os.close(rfd)
        os.close(wfd)
        log_fh.write("failed to run apt-get: %s\n" % exc)
        return 127, False
    os.close(wfd)

    cancelled = False
    buf = b""
    stdin_fd = sys.stdin.fileno() if allow_cancel else None
    try:
        while True:
            fds = [rfd]
            if stdin_fd is not None:
                fds.append(stdin_fd)
            try:
                ready, _, _ = select.select(fds, [], [], 0.5)
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                ready = []
            if rfd in ready:
                chunk = os.read(rfd, 4096)
                if chunk:
                    buf += chunk
                    while b"\n" in buf:
                        raw, buf = buf.split(b"\n", 1)
                        parse_status_line(raw.decode("utf-8", "replace"), stage)
                else:
                    break  # writer closed: apt exited
            if stdin_fd is not None and stdin_fd in ready:
                try:
                    data = os.read(stdin_fd, 4096)
                except OSError:
                    data = b""
                if not data:
                    stdin_fd = None  # UI went away; keep going safely
                elif b"CANCEL" in data and not cancelled:
                    cancelled = True
                    log_fh.write("cancel requested by user\n")
                    log_fh.flush()
                    proc.send_signal(signal.SIGINT)
            if proc.poll() is not None and rfd not in ready:
                # Drain whatever the status pipe still holds, then stop.
                os.set_blocking(rfd, False)
                try:
                    while True:
                        chunk = os.read(rfd, 4096)
                        if not chunk:
                            break
                        buf += chunk
                except OSError:
                    pass
                for raw in buf.split(b"\n"):
                    if raw:
                        parse_status_line(raw.decode("utf-8", "replace"), stage)
                break
    finally:
        try:
            os.close(rfd)
        except OSError:
            pass
    rc = proc.wait()
    log_fh.write("apt-get exited %d\n" % rc)
    log_fh.flush()
    return rc, cancelled


# ---- snapper / Phoenix Point discovery -----------------------------------

def root_is_btrfs():
    """Filesystem type of / read from the KERNEL.

    This decides one thing and one thing only: WHICH SENTENCE the Welcome
    failure card shows, "no Phoenix Point, rollback unavailable on this
    filesystem" against the generic one.  Whether a Point exists and which
    number it is are decided by sfupdate.snapshots and by nothing here.  The
    fact is read from /proc/mounts rather than from findmnt for the reason
    phoenix.trusted gives: a layout fact taken from the kernel cannot be
    substituted, so it needs no trust classification.
    """
    try:
        with open("/proc/mounts", "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                parts = line.split()
                if len(parts) >= 3 and parts[1] == "/":
                    return parts[2] == "btrfs"
    except OSError:
        pass
    return False


def phoenix_baseline():
    """Highest snapshot number before this transaction, or None.

    None means "no Point can come out of this install", and it is what stops
    the code below from looking for one.  Both facts - can this machine
    produce a Point, and what is the highest number now - belong to
    sfupdate.snapshots.  This program owns no snapper parser and runs no
    snapper: it used to own both, and the number it derived went straight to
    `pkexec /usr/libexec/phoenix-restore` while Fireproof went on naming a
    different one.
    """
    if sfsnap is None or not sfsnap.phoenix_available():
        return None
    return sfsnap.max_number()


def bundle_change_set_hash(catalog_id, pkgs):
    """The change-set hash for this bundle, namespaced so it cannot collide.

    fireproofd's change_set_hash is a bare sha256 hexdigest of an apt change
    set, and a hash recorded here lands in the same failed_sets history.  The
    `bundle:` prefix is what stops a rolled-back BUNDLE from ever being read
    as a rolled-back UPGRADE and suppressing a legitimate update.
    """
    body = "\n".join([catalog_id] + sorted(pkgs))
    return "bundle:" + hashlib.sha256(body.encode()).hexdigest()


def record_transaction(catalog_id, pkgs, point, commit_ok, log_fh):
    """Write this install into Fireproof's ONE update record.  True on success.

    THE DEFECT THIS EXISTS TO CLOSE, as measured before the fix: a bundle
    install committed packages as root and told nobody, so with a previous
    Fireproof update recorded at Point 41 this program printed SF-POINT 43
    while Fireproof1.RollbackTarget still answered 41 - the rollback button
    restoring the machine to before the UPDATE, silently discarding an install
    the user had just finished, and the two surfaces disagreeing by two Points.

    The record goes through sfupdate.state in the shape fireproofd writes,
    because a second copy of the writer is the same defect one level down.
    Two fields are this program's own and both are there to keep the record
    honest rather than uniform:

      source   which program committed.  A reader that assumes every record is
               an upgrade is then wrong in a way it can see.
      verdict  "not-verified" on success: this program runs no verify battery
               and inventing "ok" would be the forged-clean-scan shape.  On a
               failed commit it is "restore-recommended", which is not a
               judgement of the machine but the same evidence fireproofd acts
               on - the commit did not complete.

    fireproof-pending is deliberately NOT written; see the module docstring.

    Returns "recorded", or the SF-NOPOINT reason why it did not.
    """
    if sfstate is None:
        return "no-authority"
    if point is None:
        return "no-snapshot"
    # WHILE AN UPDATE IS STILL PENDING, THIS RECORDS NOTHING.
    #
    # sfupdate.state.rollback_target takes the POINT from fireproof-pending
    # when one exists but the TXN ID from last_txn, always. That was consistent
    # while fireproofd wrote both. Overwriting last_txn here between a commit
    # and its first good boot splits them: RollbackTarget would then offer the
    # UPDATE's Point carrying the BUNDLE's txn id, and the RecordRollback that
    # follows a restore would file the bundle's change-set hash in failed_sets
    # instead of the update's - so the update that had just been rolled back
    # would be offered again with no "Don't proceed" and no warning. That is
    # the failed_sets suppression, defeated.
    #
    # The correct rollback target in this window is the update's Point anyway:
    # a desktop that does not come back may be the update's doing, and its
    # Point is the one that repairs that. So nothing is recorded and no Point
    # is offered - the bundle is undone by the update's restore, and no two
    # surfaces name different numbers.
    pending = sfstate.read_pending(PENDING_FILE)
    if pending is not None:
        log_fh.write("an update is pending (txn %s, Point %s); this install's "
                     "Point %d is not recorded and not offered\n"
                     % (pending.get("txn"), pending.get("pre_point"), point))
        return "pending-update"
    try:
        state = sfstate.load_state(STATE_FILE)
        state["last_txn"] = {
            "txn": str(uuid.uuid4()),
            "hash": bundle_change_set_hash(catalog_id, pkgs),
            "date": sfstate.now_iso(),
            "pre_point": point,
            "verdict": "not-verified" if commit_ok else "restore-recommended",
            "kernels": [],
            "source": SOURCE,
            "bundle": catalog_id,
        }
        sfstate.save_state(state, STATE_FILE)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        log_fh.write("could not record the transaction: %s\n" % exc)
        return "not-recorded"
    return "recorded"


# ---- post-config steps (data-driven, known tokens only) ------------------

def post_config_audio_realtime(log_fh):
    """Pro Audio config step: realtime limits + pipewire group membership."""
    try:
        atomic_write(
            LIMITS_FILE,
            "# Installed by Shadowfetch Ignition (Pro Audio).\n"
            "# Realtime scheduling and memory locking for PipeWire pro audio.\n"
            "@pipewire   -   rtprio    95\n"
            "@pipewire   -   memlock   unlimited\n",
        )
        log_fh.write("wrote %s\n" % LIMITS_FILE)
    except OSError as exc:
        log_fh.write("could not write %s: %s\n" % (LIMITS_FILE, exc))
    uid = os.environ.get("PKEXEC_UID") or os.environ.get("SUDO_UID")
    if not uid or not uid.isdigit():
        log_fh.write("no invoking user known; skipping group membership\n")
        return
    try:
        user = pwd.getpwuid(int(uid)).pw_name
    except KeyError:
        log_fh.write("uid %s has no passwd entry; skipping group\n" % uid)
        return
    try:
        grp.getgrnam("pipewire")
    except KeyError:
        log_fh.write("no pipewire group on this system; skipping\n")
        return
    gpasswd = trusted_program(GPASSWD_PATHS)
    if gpasswd is None:
        # The group carries realtime scheduling and unlimited memory locking.
        # No trusted gpasswd means the membership is not granted, not that
        # some other gpasswd grants it.
        log_fh.write("no trusted gpasswd at %s; skipping group membership\n"
                     % ", ".join(GPASSWD_PATHS))
        return
    r = subprocess.run([gpasswd, "-a", user, "pipewire"],
                       capture_output=True, text=True, env=APT_ENV)
    log_fh.write("%s -a %s pipewire -> %d\n" % (gpasswd, user, r.returncode))


POST_CONFIG = {"audio-realtime": post_config_audio_realtime}


# ---- the install verb ----------------------------------------------------

def verb_install(catalog_id):
    if os.geteuid() != 0:
        die(7, "not-root",
            "the install verb must run as root (pkexec %s install <id>)"
            % sys.argv[0])
    rec = load_record(catalog_id)
    pkgs = list(rec["packages"])
    is_ignition = catalog_id.startswith("ignition-")
    ensure_dirs()

    ts = time.strftime("%Y%m%d-%H%M%S")
    prefix = "ignition" if is_ignition else "catalog"
    log_path = os.path.join(LOG_DIR, "%s-%s.log" % (prefix, ts))
    log_fh = open(log_path, "a", encoding="utf-8")
    os.chmod(log_path, 0o644)
    emit("LOG", log_path)
    log_fh.write("=== shadowfetch-bundle-install %s (%s) ===\n"
                 % (catalog_id, ts))

    if not pkgs:
        # Core is empty by construction: nothing to download, nothing to
        # install; completing it is a state write only.
        log_fh.write("empty package list; completing without a transaction\n")
        if is_ignition:
            atomic_write(DONE_FILE, "completed " +
                         datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")
                         + " id=" + catalog_id + "\n")
            try:
                os.unlink(CHOICE_FILE)
            except OSError:
                pass
        emit("DONE")
        log_fh.close()
        return

    # Full Flame pre-flight: refuse in plain words, naming the number.
    if catalog_id == "ignition-full-flame":
        vfs = os.statvfs("/")
        free_gb = (vfs.f_bavail * vfs.f_frsize) / (1024 ** 3)
        if free_gb < 5.0:
            log_fh.write("free space %.1f GB < 5 GB; refusing\n" % free_gb)
            log_fh.close()
            die(4, "no-space",
                "Full Flame needs at least 5 GB of free disk space and this "
                "disk has %.1f GB free." % free_gb)

    # Politeness: if Discover/pkcon/terminal apt holds the lock, wait.
    if not wait_for_locks(allow_cancel=True):
        log_fh.close()
        sys.exit(10)

    apt_get = trusted_program(APT_GET_PATHS)
    if apt_get is None:
        log_fh.close()
        die(6, "no-apt",
            "no trusted apt-get at %s - nothing was installed"
            % ", ".join(APT_GET_PATHS))
    dpkg = trusted_program(DPKG_PATHS)
    if dpkg is None:
        log_fh.close()
        die(6, "no-dpkg",
            "no trusted dpkg at %s - nothing was installed"
            % ", ".join(DPKG_PATHS))

    # If a previous transaction died mid-dpkg, repair before adding to it.
    audit = subprocess.run([dpkg, "--audit"], capture_output=True,
                           text=True, env=APT_ENV)
    if audit.stdout.strip():
        log_fh.write("dpkg --audit non-empty; running apt-get -f install\n")
        emit("STAGE", "commit")
        emit("PROGRESS", "commit 0.0 Repairing an interrupted installation")
        rc, _ = run_apt([apt_get, "-y", "-f", "install"], "commit",
                        log_fh, allow_cancel=False)
        if rc != 0:
            log_fh.close()
            die(6, "repair-failed",
                "could not repair the previous interrupted installation; "
                "see the log")

    # STAGE 1 - download only. Cancel or network loss changes nothing on
    # disk, and partially downloaded debs resume next time.
    emit("STAGE", "download")
    rc, cancelled = run_apt(
        [apt_get, "-y", "-d", "-o", "DPkg::Lock::Timeout=120", "install"]
        + pkgs,
        "download", log_fh, allow_cancel=True)
    if cancelled:
        emit("CANCELLED")
        log_fh.close()
        sys.exit(10)
    if rc != 0:
        log_fh.close()
        die(5, "download-failed",
            "the download did not complete - nothing was changed on disk, "
            "and it will resume where it stopped")

    # Baseline for Phoenix Point discovery, from the ONE authority. The
    # 79phoenix-space and 80snapper DPkg hooks wrap the commit in pre/post
    # snapshots on their own; the first pre snapshot after this baseline is
    # THE Point for the whole transaction. This helper creates no snapshots
    # and relabels none.
    baseline = phoenix_baseline()

    # STAGE 2 - install from the local cache. No cancel: interrupting dpkg
    # is the one thing that can genuinely hurt, so this stage is
    # "finishing - safe to leave running".
    emit("STAGE", "commit")
    rc, _ = run_apt(
        [apt_get, "-y", "-o", "DPkg::Lock::Timeout=120", "install"] + pkgs,
        "commit", log_fh, allow_cancel=False)

    # RECORD FIRST, OFFER SECOND. A Point that only this program knows about
    # is the whole defect: the failure card below turns it into
    # `pkexec /usr/libexec/phoenix-restore <n>` while Fireproof keeps naming
    # the previous transaction. Whatever cannot be recorded is not named.
    point = sfsnap.first_pre_after(baseline) if baseline is not None else None
    outcome = record_transaction(catalog_id, pkgs, point, rc == 0, log_fh)
    if outcome == "recorded":
        log_fh.write("recorded pre_point %d in %s\n" % (point, STATE_FILE))
        emit("POINT", str(point))
    elif point is not None:
        # pending-update / not-recorded / no-authority: a Point exists and is
        # deliberately not named, because nothing recorded it.
        emit("NOPOINT", outcome)
    elif sfsnap is None:
        emit("NOPOINT", "no-authority")
    elif not root_is_btrfs():
        emit("NOPOINT", "not-btrfs")
    elif baseline is None:
        emit("NOPOINT", "unavailable")
    else:
        emit("NOPOINT", "no-snapshot")

    if rc != 0:
        audit = subprocess.run([dpkg, "--audit"], capture_output=True,
                               text=True, env=APT_ENV)
        log_fh.write("dpkg --audit after failure:\n" + audit.stdout + "\n")
        if audit.stdout.strip():
            emit("AUDIT-DIRTY")
        log_fh.close()
        die(6, "commit-failed", "the installation did not complete")

    # Post-config steps are data-driven but id-locked: only tokens this
    # helper knows are ever executed.
    for token in rec.get("post_config") or []:
        fn = POST_CONFIG.get(token)
        if fn is not None:
            fn(log_fh)
        else:
            log_fh.write("unknown post_config token ignored: %r\n" % token)

    if is_ignition:
        atomic_write(DONE_FILE, "completed " +
                     datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")
                     + " id=" + catalog_id + "\n")
        try:
            os.unlink(CHOICE_FILE)
        except OSError:
            pass

    log_fh.write("success\n")
    log_fh.close()
    emit("DONE")


# ---- entry ---------------------------------------------------------------

def main():
    args = sys.argv[1:]
    state_only = False
    if args and args[0] == "--state-entry":
        # Reached through /usr/libexec/shadowfetch-ignition-state, whose
        # polkit action is passwordless for active local users; that entry
        # point may only write the two tiny state files, never install.
        state_only = True
        args = args[1:]
    if not args:
        usage()
    verb, rest = args[0], args[1:]

    if verb == "install":
        if state_only:
            die(7, "verb-not-allowed",
                "install is not available through the state entry point")
        if len(rest) != 1:
            usage()
        verb_install(rest[0])
    elif verb == "save-choice":
        if len(rest) != 1:
            usage()
        if os.geteuid() != 0:
            die(7, "not-root",
                "run via pkexec /usr/libexec/shadowfetch-ignition-state "
                "save-choice <id>")
        verb_save_choice(rest[0])
    elif verb == "mark-done":
        if rest:
            usage()
        if os.geteuid() != 0:
            die(7, "not-root",
                "run via pkexec /usr/libexec/shadowfetch-ignition-state "
                "mark-done")
        verb_mark_done()
    else:
        usage()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
